    {
      "args": [[[1, 2, 9], [5, 9, 9], [1, 1, 1]]],
      "expected": 9,
      "explanation": "Always taking the cheaper next square starts 1 → 2, then is stuck between two 9s and ends with at least 14. The best lane pays 5 early to reach the easy bottom row: 1 + 5 + 1 + 1 + 1 = 9."
    }
  ],
  "hints": [
    "Look at the gate square in the bottom-right corner. The lane reached it from one of only two squares. Which ones, and which would you rather have come from?",
    "It came from the square above or the square to the left. If you knew the cheapest time to reach each of those, the cheapest time to reach the gate is its own minutes plus the smaller of the two.",
    "That works for every square: <code>best(r, c) = snow[r][c] + min(best(r - 1, c), best(r, c - 1))</code>. Squares on the top row only have a left neighbour, and squares on the left edge only have one above.",
    "Fill the table row by row, left to right, so both neighbours are ready when you need them. A single row of values is enough: before you overwrite <code>best[c]</code>, it still holds the value from the row above."
  ],
