  "solution": {
    "js": [
      "function minClearingTime(snow) {",
      "  const cols = snow[0].length;",
      "  const best = new Array(cols).fill(Infinity); // best[c] for the current row",
      "  best[0] = 0;",
      "  for (const row of snow) {",
      "    best[0] += row[0]; // left edge: only from above",
      "    for (let c = 1; c < cols; c++) {",
      "      // best[c] still holds the row above; best[c - 1] is the square on the left",
      "      best[c] = row[c] + Math.min(best[c], best[c - 1]);",
      "    }",
      "  }",
      "  return best[cols - 1];",
      "}"
    ],
    "py": [
      "def min_clearing_time(snow):",
      "    cols = len(snow[0])",
      "    best = [float(\"inf\")] * cols  # best[c] for the current row",
      "    best[0] = 0",
      "    for row in snow:",
      "        best[0] += row[0]  # left edge: only from above",
      "        for c in range(1, cols):",
      "            # best[c] still holds the row above; best[c - 1] is the square on the left",
      "            best[c] = row[c] + min(best[c], best[c - 1])",
      "    return best[cols - 1]"
    ],
    "walkthrough": [
      "<p>The cheapest lane to any square must arrive from above or from the left, and the part before that must itself be the cheapest lane to that neighbour. So <code>best(r, c) = snow[r][c] + min(above, left)</code>.</p>",
      "<p>Trace the first example, one row at a time:</p>",
      "<ol>",
      "<li>Row 0 <code>[2, 1, 4]</code>: only from the left, so <code>2, 3, 7</code>.</li>",
      "<li>Row 1 <code>[3, 8, 1]</code>: 2 + 3 = 5; then 8 + min(3, 5) = 11; then 1 + min(7, 11) = 8.</li>",
      "<li>Row 2 <code>[6, 2, 1]</code>: 5 + 6 = 11; then 2 + min(11, 11) = 13; then 1 + min(8, 13) = 9.</li>",
      "</ol>",
      "<p>The answer is 9. The code starts every cell at infinity so the first row works without a special case: on row 0, &quot;above&quot; is infinity and the left neighbour always wins.</p>"
    ],
    "time": "O(rows × cols)",
